This repository commits age ciphertext and its remote is public. That is a
decision, not an accident, and this file is the record of it.

What is committed
    deploy/secrets/claude-credentials.json.age

What opens it
    AGE_PRIVATE_KEY, a Fly secret, and ~/.config/hermes/age-key.txt on the
    founder's laptop. Neither is in this repository, and .gitignore refuses the
    filename, so a clone gets the envelope and nothing that opens it.

Why it is here
    the Fly-era entrypoint (deleted 2026-08-27, R1) decrypted it to a scratch file on every boot, compared
    the token against the one in use, logs one line and deletes it. Shipping the
    ciphertext inside the image is what makes that fallback testable on every
    boot rather than hoped for.

What this accepts
    A public git object is permanent. It stays fetchable by sha after every
    branch is gone, so if the age key ever leaks this ciphertext is readable
    retroactively and cannot be recalled. The credential inside it is therefore
    treated as rotatable and is rotated, never as a long-lived secret.

Withdraw the allowance by deleting this file. The estate commit gate refuses the
next encrypted store the moment it is gone.
